---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'CCA vs CA vs MCA: The Temperature Ladder Behind Cranking-Amp Ratings';
const description = 'CCA, CA, MCA and HCA all measure cranking amps, but at different temperatures. CCA is at -18°C, CA/MCA at 0°C, and HCA at 27°C — so the same battery prints a different, higher number at each warmer test.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/cca/cca-vs-ca/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Cranking amp ratings (CCA, CA, MCA, HCA)',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-14',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between CCA and CA?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Temperature. CCA (cold cranking amps) is measured at −18°C (0°F); CA (cranking amps) at 0°C (32°F). Because CA is tested warmer, the CA number is always higher than the CCA number for the same battery.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is MCA the same as CA?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'For practical purposes yes — MCA (marine cranking amps) and CA are both measured at 0°C (32°F) for 30 seconds to 7.2V. The difference is the label&rsquo;s intended market (marine vs automotive), not the test.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How much higher is MCA than CCA?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Typically about 20–25% higher, because MCA is tested at 0°C rather than −18°C. A battery rated 800 MCA is roughly a 640 CCA battery — the difference is the temperature, not the battery.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-14"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · CCA Cluster · Last reviewed 2026-09-14</p>
      <h1>CCA vs CA vs MCA: The Temperature Ladder Behind Cranking-Amp Ratings</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        CCA, CA, MCA and HCA all measure the same thing — cranking power — but at <strong>different
        temperatures</strong>. The only thing that changes is how cold the test is, and that single variable is
        why one battery can carry four different amp ratings on its label.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> These are the same test at different temperatures. <strong>CCA</strong> is measured at −18°C (0°F); <strong>CA</strong> and <strong>MCA</strong> at 0°C (32°F); <strong>HCA</strong> at 27°C (80°F). A warmer test makes the battery deliver more current, so the number climbs as you move down the ladder — a battery's MCA is always higher than its CCA, and its HCA higher still.
      </div>

      <h2>The four ratings at a glance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Rating</th>
            <th>Full name</th>
            <th>Test temperature</th>
            <th>Where it appears</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>CCA</strong></td>
            <td>Cold Cranking Amps</td>
            <td>−18°C (0°F)</td>
            <td>Automotive batteries (cold-climate)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>CA</strong></td>
            <td>Cranking Amps</td>
            <td>0°C (32°F)</td>
            <td>Older / warm-climate labels</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>MCA</strong></td>
            <td>Marine Cranking Amps</td>
            <td>0°C (32°F)</td>
            <td>Marine and powersports batteries</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>HCA</strong></td>
            <td>Hot Cranking Amps</td>
            <td>27°C (80°F)</td>
            <td>Warm-climate / seasonal ratings</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        All four use the same 30-second, 7.2V-cutoff method (SAE J537). The only variable that changes is
        temperature — and that is the whole story, because a battery's ability to deliver current drops as it
        gets colder.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the number climbs as the test warms</h2>
      <p>
        Cold slows a battery's electrochemistry and raises its internal resistance, so at −18°C it can deliver
        less current than at 0°C. Test the same battery warmer and you measure a bigger number — not because
        the battery improved, but because you asked it an easier question. This is why <strong>MCA ≈ 1.2–1.25 × CCA</strong>:
        the 18-degree difference (0°C vs −18°C) is worth roughly 20–25% more current.
      </p>
      <p>
        An "800 MCA" marine battery is, in cold-starting terms, about a <strong>640 CCA</strong> car battery. The label is
        not lying; it is just using a warmer yardstick.
      </p>

      <h2>The practical trap: comparing across the ladder</h2>
      <p>
        The trap is identical to the JIS-vs-EN trap covered in <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">What is CCA</a>, but
        within a single market. A buyer comparing a "750 MCA" marine battery against a "700 CCA" car battery and
        concluding the marine one is stronger has made a category error — the marine number is inflated by the
        warmer test. To compare them honestly, convert MCA to CCA by dividing by roughly 1.2–1.25, then compare.
      </p>

      <h2>When each rating actually matters</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CCA</strong> — cold-climate automotive; the number to trust when a hard cold start is the requirement.</li>
        <li><strong>CA / MCA</strong> — warm climates and marine use, where 0°C is a realistic worst case and the higher label reflects a milder assumption.</li>
        <li><strong>HCA</strong> — warm-climate sizing; rarely the number you should buy against, since a real engine still has to start on a cool morning.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Common misconceptions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"MCA and CCA are interchangeable."</strong> No — MCA is tested warmer, so it is always the higher of the two for the same battery.</li>
        <li><strong>"A higher number is a stronger battery."</strong> Only within the same rating. A 750 MCA battery is not stronger than a 700 CCA battery.</li>
        <li><strong>"HCA is a stricter test."</strong> The opposite — it is the easiest test (warmest), which is why the number is highest.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>CCA (−18°C), CA/MCA (0°C) and HCA (27°C) are the <strong>same test at different temperatures</strong>.</li>
          <li>Warmer test → higher number: <strong>MCA ≈ 1.2–1.25 × CCA</strong>, and HCA higher still.</li>
          <li>To compare across the ladder, <strong>convert to one rating</strong> (divide MCA by ~1.2–1.25 to get CCA) before judging.</li>
          <li>Buy against <strong>CCA for cold climates</strong>, CA/MCA for warm or marine use.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The Editor's View</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My read:</strong> Comparing CCA against MCA is the marine world's version of the JIS-vs-EN trap —
          and the fix is the same one question: "which temperature is this number measured at?"
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Supporting logic:</strong> A label that switches from CCA to MCA is doing one of two things: honestly matching
          a marine market, or quietly making a modest battery look bigger. Either way, the buyer who knows that
          MCA runs about 20–25% hotter (and therefore 20–25% higher) can read past the label to the actual
          cold-start capability — which is, on a cold morning, the only number that matters.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          <em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-verified from primary standards and secondary references; the 0°C vs −18°C relationship is
        consistent across sources, with the exact MCA-to-CCA multiplier stated as an approximate range (1.2–1.25×)
        because it varies slightly by battery design.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>SAE J537 — cranking-ampere method (CCA −18°C, CA 0°C, HCA 27°C).</li>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — cranking-rating definitions.</li>
        <li>Jump Charge HQ, Power Curious and marine battery guides — CCA/CA/MCA comparison and the ~20–25% MCA-vs-CCA difference.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-14</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
